#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105367 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "e7c527c9-2dc1-50b2-bbf6-10c1a9d122dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10837" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n1. The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n2. The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n3. The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/0018f659e35a580657f383181bfabb0d.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots$, which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty)$.\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $[MATH: (0, 1\\,000].]$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "295d2025-c958-55e3-a194-5cdb271a510c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10838" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n${\\frac{3}{8}(x^{2} + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}-1 < x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1347/Source/Images/3c1b36806fc8009a30d99e37f36e5336.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx|= ∫_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}(x^{2} + 1)dx; = \\frac{3}{8}∫_{-1}^{1}x^{2} + 1dx; = \\frac{3}{8}[\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + x]_{-1}^{1}; = \\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1^{3}}{3} + 1] - [((-1)^{3})/(3) + (-1)]); = \\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3} + 1] - [- \\frac{1}{3} - 1]); = \\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3} - (-\\frac{4}{3})); = \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{8}{3}; = 1$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "32131e34-3141-51b3-8d26-6cee481f168c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11037" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n${2x^{3} + kx^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(2x^{3} + kx^{2})dx &= [\\frac{x^{4}}{2} + \\frac{kx^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{k}{3}] - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{3} + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1cad6f-54b0-59f7-a27c-07502506dcdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105246} {:answer/id #uuid "d41320f0-6d5f-54a0-9a41-0e8cdd683431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105247} {:answer/id #uuid "e06d0fa1-e4cf-5240-be12-319ced372bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105248} {:answer/id #uuid "1d772218-2091-5cc8-9c3d-eb87302490bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105249} {:answer/id #uuid "2006a334-d98b-5871-bbd2-4845c64d439d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d41320f0-6d5f-54a0-9a41-0e8cdd683431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105247} :answer-field/id #uuid "cebe3bcd-dd4e-51f7-be2d-f81cdcb8aa80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105245}] :question/id #uuid "70ebefad-2f9e-5da5-8283-6d6972fbe689" :question/math-academy-id "q-86911" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n1. The percent concentration of fruit concentrate in a randomly selected fruit drink\n2. The number of times a coin is flipped until it lands on tails\n3. The number of days in a particular week"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91de6dfc-3550-5227-826f-617909d90dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105348} {:answer/id #uuid "becf5a88-f447-5bbc-9a00-579a74ecfe2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105349} {:answer/id #uuid "e6038154-3664-5600-a7d9-77e6082263e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105350} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} {:answer/id #uuid "450ea2f9-252f-5675-b796-03caa076d7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5fddf9-5def-5d25-b2b2-98d44685cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3d8b68-2035-520d-82f9-f67ea75460d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105347}] :question/id #uuid "57ead9aa-937c-57a1-8fa5-6d07fce39ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98912" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{k}{x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\le x \\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$."}]}
